(registered 2026-10-26, last updated 2026-10-26)

Type name: text

Subtype name: vnd.geml

Required parameters: N/A

Optional parameters:

charset -- if present, MUST be "UTF-8". See "Additional information".

Encoding considerations: binary

GEML content is UTF-8 text. In the canonical form required of text/*
types by RFC 2046 section 4.1.1, line breaks are CRLF.

Security considerations:

A GEML document is frequently machine-generated and frequently
untrusted: it may arrive from a language model, a pipeline, or a pull
request. Section 9 of the specification is normative about what a
processor must guarantee when the document is hostile.

Documents are data, never code. A processor MUST NOT execute or
evaluate any part of a document: a code block's body is stored text
and MUST NOT be run; a diagram body is passed verbatim to an external
renderer and is never interpreted by the processor; there is no
raw-HTML escape hatch and no expression language beyond a closed table
arithmetic that has no conditionals, no lookups, no cross-table
references and no embedded program by construction.

Document-controlled text is untrusted. Block ids, class names and
attribute values all come from the document. A processor MUST NOT
construct a regular expression, a shell command, or any other
executable form from such text without escaping it for the target
grammar.

Resource exhaustion. A processor MUST bound the depth to which it
recurses over typed-block nesting, list nesting and inline nesting,
emit the corresponding diagnostic on reaching a bound, and continue;
it MUST NOT overflow its call stack or fail to produce a model.

Non-termination. Internal references cannot loop, because ids are
unique within a document and resolution is a lookup. Cross-document
reference checking is performed exactly one level deep, so two
documents that reference each other terminate. Content transclusion is
recursive, and a processor MUST track the chain of expanded documents
and stop with a transclusion-cycle error. Computed table columns see
only earlier columns, making the dependency graph acyclic by
construction.

Path traversal. Resolving a cross-document reference reads a file
named by the document. A processor MUST confine that resolution to an
explicitly configured root directory, MUST resolve every symbolic link
before deciding whether a target lies inside the root, and MUST refuse
a target that escapes it. Resolution MUST fail closed: a processor
that cannot establish a confinement root resolves nothing rather than
falling back to an unconfined lookup.

Disclosure through render-time fetches. A media src, and a table
source naming an http(s) URL, are fetched at render time by the
renderer and are never read by the parser. A fetched URL discloses the
reader's address, and the fact and time of reading, to whoever
controls it. A renderer SHOULD confine src to the document's own
origin or directory and SHOULD require an explicit opt-in before
performing http(s) fetches.

Dangerous sinks. A link or embed destination naming a URL scheme other
than http, https, mailto or tel MUST NOT be emitted as a navigable or
loadable target. The check MUST be applied when building the model
rather than at the rendering sink, so that every consumer of the model
inherits it, and it MUST ignore leading and embedded characters in the
range U+0000-U+0020 when determining the scheme, because user agents
strip them before acting on a URL. A renderer emitting a markup format
MUST escape document-controlled text for the position it occupies --
element text, attribute value, or URL.

Truncation. U+0000 MUST be replaced with U+FFFD, so that a document
cannot make one tool in a pipeline see less content than the parser
saw.

Charset confusion. Ill-formed byte sequences decode to U+FFFD and a
processor MUST NOT re-interpret the input in another encoding, which
removes encoding-detection as an attack surface.

Sidecar disclosure. The version sidecar described under "File
extension(s)" below is itself a GEML document and carries superseded
revisions of the document it accompanies. Publishing, mirroring or
transmitting a sidecar discloses content that was deliberately removed
from the live document. Consumers that copy directories wholesale
SHOULD treat a sidecar as carrying at least the sensitivity of every
revision it holds.

Interoperability considerations:

A GEML processor also accepts LF and bare CR and normalizes every line
ending to a single LF before parsing; the choice of line ending MUST
NOT change the document model (specification section 0.3), so
converting between the canonical form and a local line-ending
convention never changes the document. The encoding is binary rather
than 8bit because GEML imposes no line-length limit: lines longer than
998 octets are permitted. A single leading U+FEFF is removed before
parsing (section 0.2).

A processor MUST degrade gracefully on constructs it does not
recognize, so adding a block type or a diagram format is not a
breaking change. The typed block registry is open: an unregistered
type name should contain a hyphen, leaving hyphen-free names to future
versions of the specification.

An implementation may claim conformance to GEML 1.0 once it reproduces
the published conformance suite case for case. No permission or
sign-off from the project is required.

An unresolved reference is an error, so both fragment forms described
under "Fragment identifier considerations" MUST be resolvable by every
processor: a fragment one processor understands and another does not
is the same document building in one and failing in the other.

