https://github.com/geml-spec/geml/tree/main/geml-parser/test/conformance

Applications that use this media type:

The reference parser, CLI and MCP server (npm package @geml/geml,
MIT); a Visual Studio Code extension; a browser extension that renders
.geml resources in place; a tree-sitter grammar; Logseq and Obsidian
integrations; a GitHub Action that validates documents in CI; and
documentation toolchains that validate .geml at build time. Documents
are also authored and consumed programmatically by language-model
agents.

Fragment identifier considerations:

A fragment identifier on a text/vnd.geml resource denotes the block
bearing that id, matching the reference syntax of the specification:
other.geml#budget names the same block whether it is written as a GEML
reference or as a URL. Ids are unique within a document, so resolution
is a lookup rather than a traversal.

A fragment may also denote prose: a run of ordinary text, such as
paragraphs or lists, that sits between id-bearing blocks and so has no
id of its own. Its address is derived from the ids around it, with a
relation word fixed by the structure (specification section 4):
"P-between-N" for prose between P and N, "C-before-N" for prose that
opens the section or block C, and "C-after-P" for prose that closes
it. In the document

# Install {#install}

Download the archive and unpack it.

=== code {#unpack lang=sh}
tar xf geml.tar.gz
===

Then put the binary on your PATH.

=== code {#verify lang=sh}
geml --version
===

That should print the version.

#install-before-unpack denotes "Download the archive and unpack it.",
#unpack-between-verify denotes "Then put the binary on your PATH.",
and #install-after-verify denotes "That should print the version."
Because each address names both ends of the run, inserting a block
between #unpack and #verify makes #unpack-between-verify stop
resolving rather than silently naming a shorter run. A declared id
always takes precedence over a derived prose address. A processor MUST
resolve both forms.

Additional information:

Charset: A GEML document is always UTF-8 (specification section 0.1);
the charset is determined by the format, not by this parameter. The
parameter is optional despite the advice of RFC 6838 section 4.2.1,
because requiring a value that can never vary would make every sender
that omits it nonconforming while conveying no information. It is
defined at all so that the "charset=UTF-8" many HTTP servers append to
text/* responses by default is a permitted value rather than an
undefined parameter. Any other value is an error, not a request to
transcode.

Deprecated alias names for this type: N/A

Magic number(s): N/A

A GEML document is UTF-8 text. A typed block opens with a line
beginning "===", but no construct is required to appear first and a
byte order mark is permitted, so there is no reliable leading
signature.

File extension(s): "geml", "gemlhistory"

A ".gemlhistory" file is a complete, valid GEML document of the same
media type: a version sidecar that declares its vocabulary in its
own metadata block like any other document. The separate extension
marks the document's role, not its format, so that a directory walk
collecting documents can take ".geml" and leave the sidecar, whose
stored blobs hold superseded text. Neither extension is a vocabulary
declaration.

Macintosh file type code(s): TEXT

